HUB.add({
  id: 'javascript', title: 'JavaScript essentials for React', short: 'JavaScript',
  blurb: 'Added: the JS questions React interviews always mix in.',
  items: [
{ q: `What is a closure?`,
  k: `A backpack a function carries: it remembers the things that were around when it was made.`,
  a: `A closure is a function plus the variables from the scope where it was created, kept alive after that scope ends. Used for data privacy, factories, memoization, and it's how React handlers "see" props and state. It also causes <b>stale closures</b>: an effect or interval that captured an old value.`,
  c: `function counter() { let n = 0; return () => ++n }
const next = counter(); next(); next()   // 2` },
{ q: `What is the event loop?`,
  k: `A kitchen with one cook: he does one job at a time, and a ticket line tells him what's next.`,
  a: `JavaScript runs on one thread with a call stack. Async work (timers, I/O) is handled by the host; callbacks queue up. After each task, the event loop drains the <b>microtask</b> queue (promise callbacks, <code>queueMicrotask</code>) before the next <b>macrotask</b> (setTimeout, events) and before rendering.`,
  c: `console.log(1)
setTimeout(() => console.log(4))
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(3))
console.log(2)        // 1 2 3 4` },
{ q: `What is hoisting?`,
  k: `JavaScript reads your list of names first, before doing the work.`,
  a: `Declarations are processed before code runs. <code>var</code> is hoisted and set to undefined; function declarations are fully hoisted; <code>let</code>, <code>const</code> and <code>class</code> are hoisted but in the temporal dead zone until their line runs (ReferenceError if used early).` },
{ q: `How does "this" work in JavaScript?`,
  k: `"this" means "who is doing it right now", and that depends on how the job was called.`,
  a: `Determined by the call site: method call → the object; plain call → undefined in strict mode; <code>new</code> → the new instance; <code>call/apply/bind</code> → the given value. Arrow functions don't have their own <code>this</code>; they use the enclosing one, which is why they were used for class component handlers.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between == and ===?`,
  k: `== says "close enough"; === says "exactly the same".`,
  a: `<code>===</code> compares without type conversion. <code>==</code> converts types first (<code>'1' == 1</code> is true, <code>null == undefined</code> is true). Use <code>===</code>; React uses <code>Object.is</code> for state comparisons (like === but NaN equals NaN and +0 differs from -0).` },
{ q: `What are promises and async/await?`,
  k: `A promise is a ticket: "your toy will come later, or I'll tell you it couldn't".`,
  a: `A Promise represents a future value in pending, fulfilled or rejected state. <code>async</code> functions return promises; <code>await</code> pauses until one settles. Use <code>try/catch</code> for errors, <code>Promise.all</code> for parallel work (fails fast), <code>Promise.allSettled</code> for all results, <code>Promise.race/any</code> for first result.`,
  c: `const [user, orders] = await Promise.all([getUser(id), getOrders(id)])` },
{ q: `What is prototypal inheritance?`,
  k: `If a toy doesn't know a trick, it asks its parent toy, then the grandparent.`,
  a: `Objects link to a prototype object; property lookups walk up the chain until found or null. <code>class</code> is syntax over prototypes. <code>Object.create(proto)</code> creates objects with a given prototype.` },
{ q: `What are map, filter and reduce?`,
  k: `map: change every toy. filter: keep only some toys. reduce: squash all toys into one answer.`,
  a: `Non-mutating array methods returning new values: <code>map</code> transforms each item, <code>filter</code> keeps items passing a test, <code>reduce</code> folds items into one value. Central to React rendering and immutable updates. Newer non-mutating helpers: <code>toSorted</code>, <code>toReversed</code>, <code>with</code>, <code>Object.groupBy</code>.`,
  c: `const total = cart.filter(i => i.inStock).map(i => i.price * i.qty).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0)` },
{ q: `What are spread, rest and destructuring?`,
  k: `Spread pours toys out of a box; rest scoops the leftovers into a box; destructuring takes toys out by name.`,
  a: `Spread <code>...</code> expands arrays/objects (copies, merging). Rest collects remaining items into an array/object (function args, destructuring). Destructuring extracts values by position or name with defaults and renaming. Used everywhere in props and state updates.`,
  c: `const { id, ...rest } = props
const next = { ...state, filters: { ...state.filters, page: 2 } }` },
{ q: `What are optional chaining and nullish coalescing?`,
  k: `?. means "only look inside if it exists". ?? means "if there's nothing, use this instead".`,
  a: `<code>a?.b?.c</code> returns undefined instead of throwing if a link is null/undefined. <code>x ?? fallback</code> uses fallback only for null/undefined, unlike <code>||</code>, which also replaces 0, '' and false.`,
  c: `const city = user?.address?.city ?? 'Unknown'
const qty = input ?? 1   // keeps 0` },
{ q: `What are ES modules?`,
  k: `Each file is a toy box with labels saying which toys others may borrow.`,
  a: `Standard modules with <code>import</code>/<code>export</code>: static structure enables tree shaking; dynamic <code>import()</code> enables code splitting. Named vs default exports; top-level await is allowed. Bundlers and browsers both understand them.` },
{ q: `What is immutability and why does React care?`,
  k: `Instead of scribbling on the old drawing, you make a new one, so it's easy to see something changed.`,
  a: `Immutability means not changing existing objects but creating new ones. React compares by reference (<code>Object.is</code>): a new object signals a change, enabling cheap checks for re-rendering, memo and selectors. Mutating state leaves the reference unchanged, so React may skip the update.` },
{ q: `What is currying?`,
  k: `Instead of giving all ingredients at once, you give one, then the next, then the next.`,
  a: `Transforming <code>f(a, b, c)</code> into <code>f(a)(b)(c)</code>. Useful for configuring functions step by step (Redux middleware signature <code>store =&gt; next =&gt; action</code>, event handler factories).`,
  c: `const handleChange = field => e => setForm(f => ({ ...f, [field]: e.target.value }))` },
{ q: `What is memoization?`,
  k: `Writing down answers to hard sums so you don't do them twice.`,
  a: `Caching a function's results by input. Pure functions only. React's useMemo/memo/Compiler and Reselect use it; costs memory, so cache what's expensive and repeated.` },
{ q: `What are common JavaScript memory leaks in React apps?`,
  k: `Toys left running after you leave the room: timers, listeners, open phone calls.`,
  a: `Intervals/timeouts not cleared, event listeners or subscriptions not removed, unclosed sockets, global caches that grow forever, detached DOM nodes held in refs, and closures holding large data. Fix with effect cleanups, AbortController, WeakMap for caches, and the Memory panel in DevTools.` },
  ],
});
